@using MudBlazor

<div class="d-flex flex-column">
    <div class="pa-3" style="max-height:70vh; overflow-y:auto">
        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">Team capacity (story points)</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-2">
            Capacity is measured in story points. Each team sets how many story points one working day is worth
            as <b>Story points per day</b> on the
            <MudLink Href="@($"{Estimation.RoutingPath.Teams}/{TeamId}")" Target="_blank">Team page</MudLink>
            (1 by default). This team: <b>1 working day = @StoryPointsPerDay.ToString("0.##") SP</b>.
            For each active team member:
        </MudText>
        <MudPaper Elevation="0" Class="pa-2 mb-3 rounded" Style="background: var(--mud-palette-background-gray); font-family: monospace">
            member_capacity_sp = (working_days_in_window − holiday_days − public_holidays) × coefficient × story_points_per_day
        </MudPaper>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
            The team's total capacity is the sum across all active members.
        </MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-2">
            The window is the union of the team's <b>Sprints</b> for the selected PI. If no sprints are
            defined the PI's start/end dates are used instead. The window indicator chip shows which mode
            was applied (<i>Sprints</i> / <i>PI</i> / <i>None</i>).
        </MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-2">
            Working days exclude Saturdays and Sundays. Holiday days count any
            <MudLink Href="@($"{Estimation.RoutingPath.Teams}/{TeamId}/holidays")" Target="_blank">team-member holiday</MudLink>
            overlapping the window (half-day entries count as 0.5). Public holidays count the
            <MudLink Href="@($"{Estimation.RoutingPath.HolidaysSetup}")" Target="_blank">public holidays</MudLink>
            of the member's city and of its country that fall on a working day in the window; a date on
            both lists counts once. A leave day that lands on a public holiday is only subtracted once.
        </MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
            The coefficient (0.00–1.00, default 1.00) is set per team member per PI on the
            <MudLink Href="@($"{Estimation.RoutingPath.Teams}/{TeamId}/coefficients")" Target="_blank">Coefficients page</MudLink>.
            If a PI has no saved values the team's <b>Default</b> coefficients are used (and 1.00 where no Default is set).
        </MudText>

        <MudDivider Class="my-3" />

        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">Technology stacks</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-2">
            Each member is assigned to one or more of the team's <b>technology stacks</b> (on the Team page).
            A member's capacity is a single shared pool of days: if they belong to several stacks, those
            stacks draw from the <i>same</i> days, so a day spent on one stack is no longer free for another.
            That is why a multi-stack member's days appear in every stack they belong to.
        </MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
            Members who belong to no stack still count toward the team's total capacity, but their days
            cannot be reserved against any feature that asks for a stack.
        </MudText>

        <MudDivider Class="my-3" />

        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">Feature demand and reservation</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-2">
            The <b>Effort</b> chip on a card is this team's part of the feature — the team's story points
            on the feature (auto-summed from its stacks when the team uses stacks). Teams without story points
            of their own share what is left of the feature's <code>Story Points</code> after the other teams'
            estimates in equal parts, so a 21-point feature with two such teams shows 10.5 on each board; the chip
            then has a split icon.
        </MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-2">
            Capacity is reserved <b>per technology stack</b>. For each stack's story points on a feature,
            only the members assigned to that stack contribute their remaining capacity (1 effective day =
            the team's story points per day). Features are processed <b>top-to-bottom</b> and share one per-member
            ledger, so capacity consumed by a higher card is no longer available to the cards below it.
        </MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
            The <b>Reserved x / y</b> chip shows how much was reserved (<i>x</i>) against how much the
            feature needs (<i>y</i>). A feature with no team-stack story points shows
            <b>No stack capacity</b>: its effort is still displayed, but nothing can be reserved for it.
        </MudText>

        <MudDivider Class="my-3" />

        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">Resource and Demand tables</MudText>
        <ul style="margin: 4px 0 12px 24px; padding:0">
            <li>
                <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
                    <b>Resource capacity</b> — the supply side: each stack (or exact stack combination) of
                    members, their total story points, how many are already <i>Allocated</i>, and what
                    <i>Remains</i>. Every member is counted in exactly one row, so the rows sum to the team total.
                </MudText>
            </li>
            <li>
                <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
                    <b>Demand capacity</b> — the feature side: the story points each stack is asked for by
                    above-the-line features, how much was <i>Allocated</i> (capped by member availability),
                    the unmet remainder, and how much sits <i>below the line</i>.
                </MudText>
            </li>
        </ul>

        <MudDivider Class="my-3" />

        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">Card coloring</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-1">
            Each included card is colored by how much of its demand could be reserved:
        </MudText>
        <ul style="margin: 4px 0 12px 24px; padding:0">
            <li><MudText Typo="Typo.body2"><b style="color:var(--mud-palette-success)">Green</b> — fully reserved: every stack got all the days it needed.</MudText></li>
            <li><MudText Typo="Typo.body2"><b style="color:var(--mud-palette-warning)">Yellow</b> — partly reserved: some days were found, but not enough to cover the demand.</MudText></li>
            <li><MudText Typo="Typo.body2"><b style="color:var(--mud-palette-error)">Red</b> — nothing could be reserved: the stacks it needs were already exhausted by the cards above it.</MudText></li>
            <li><MudText Typo="Typo.body2"><b style="color:var(--mud-palette-warning)">Dashed amber</b> — <i>No stack capacity</i>: the feature has no team-stack story points, so it can't be planned.</MudText></li>
            <li><MudText Typo="Typo.body2">Features moved <b>below the line</b> with the square button next to the position consume no capacity.</MudText></li>
        </ul>

        <MudDivider Class="my-3" />

        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">Example</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-2">
            A team has two stacks: <b>Backend</b> (Alice, Bob) and <b>Frontend</b> (Carol). For the selected
            PI each member has <b>10 effective days</b> — so Backend has 20 days, Frontend 10, and the team
            total is 30. Four features sit above the line, in this order:
        </MudText>
        <div style="overflow-x:auto">
            <table class="help-example-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Feature</th>
                        <th>Demand</th>
                        <th>Reserved</th>
                        <th>Result</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>F1</td>
                        <td>Backend 15</td>
                        <td>15 / 15</td>
                        <td><b style="color:var(--mud-palette-success)">Green</b> — Backend has 20 days, easily covered (5 left).</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>F2</td>
                        <td>Backend 8, Frontend 4</td>
                        <td>9 / 12</td>
                        <td><b style="color:var(--mud-palette-warning)">Yellow</b> — only 5 Backend days remain (reserves 5); Frontend covers its 4.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>F3</td>
                        <td>Backend 6</td>
                        <td>0 / 6</td>
                        <td><b style="color:var(--mud-palette-error)">Red</b> — Backend is already exhausted.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>F4</td>
                        <td>—</td>
                        <td>—</td>
                        <td><b style="color:var(--mud-palette-warning)">No stack capacity</b> — has story points but no team stack.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.caption" Class="mud-text-secondary mt-2 d-block">
            F1 consuming Backend days is exactly why F2 is only partial and F3 is starved — the cards share
            one pool, top to bottom. Reorder them (drag a card, use the arrows, or type a position) to change who gets the days first.
        </MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.caption" Class="mud-text-secondary mt-2 d-block">
            The example uses 1 story point per day. With 2 story points per day the same 10 effective days give
            each member 20 story points, so Backend has 40 and Frontend 20, and F1, F2 and F3 are all fully reserved.
        </MudText>

        <MudDivider Class="my-3" />

        <MudText Typo="Typo.caption" Class="mud-text-secondary">
            Both capacity and feature demand are measured in story points (1 working day = the team's story
            points per day), so reserved story points can be compared directly to the team's capacity.
        </MudText>
    </div>

    <MudDivider />

    <div class="d-flex justify-end pa-3">
        <MudButton Variant="Variant.Filled" Color="Color.Primary" OnClick="Close">Close</MudButton>
    </div>
</div>

<style>
    .help-example-table {
        width: 100%;
        border-collapse: collapse;
        font-size: 0.8rem;
    }

    .help-example-table th,
    .help-example-table td {
        padding: 6px 8px;
        border: 1px solid var(--mud-palette-lines-default);
        text-align: left;
        vertical-align: top;
    }

    .help-example-table thead th {
        background: var(--mud-palette-background-gray);
        font-weight: 600;
    }
</style>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter] private IMudDialogInstance? MudDialog { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public int TeamId { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public decimal StoryPointsPerDay { get; set; } = 1m;

    private void Close()
    {
        MudDialog?.Close();
    }
}
